[{:db/id 17592186119846 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4780154a-e6c5-5212-9585-41f4b2c7d38a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4780154a-e6c5-5212-9585-41f4b2c7d38a" :question/id #uuid "4780154a-e6c5-5212-9585-41f4b2c7d38a" :question/math-academy-id "q-34832" :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 & 15 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\})=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0fd465c3-4977-5d35-b197-a5e9f9656a35"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0fd465c3-4977-5d35-b197-a5e9f9656a35" :answer-field/id #uuid "0fd465c3-4977-5d35-b197-a5e9f9656a35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a841feda-cb6b-5b98-990a-d1a0abf6cfb1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-78554db1-b432-51b4-936b-3b8941909583" "answer-ff9dadc2-a499-5eb3-a904-a3da6ea8a443" "answer-a841feda-cb6b-5b98-990a-d1a0abf6cfb1" "answer-3a113c4d-a0ac-5651-906e-41d84f5d836e" "answer-5368a6fd-1c3c-5ec5-98c7-f3629d82df88"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78554db1-b432-51b4-936b-3b8941909583" :answer/id #uuid "78554db1-b432-51b4-936b-3b8941909583" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff9dadc2-a499-5eb3-a904-a3da6ea8a443" :answer/id #uuid "ff9dadc2-a499-5eb3-a904-a3da6ea8a443" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a841feda-cb6b-5b98-990a-d1a0abf6cfb1" :answer/id #uuid "a841feda-cb6b-5b98-990a-d1a0abf6cfb1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a113c4d-a0ac-5651-906e-41d84f5d836e" :answer/id #uuid "3a113c4d-a0ac-5651-906e-41d84f5d836e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5368a6fd-1c3c-5ec5-98c7-f3629d82df88" :answer/id #uuid "5368a6fd-1c3c-5ec5-98c7-f3629d82df88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186321062 :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $1$ pivot column (or alternatively, it has exactly $1$ non-zero row). \n\nTherefore, \n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\})=1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119845 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f427e4e2-13a5-5d7b-8b8d-103e6b378823"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186348199 :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\})=$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\})=2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-f427e4e2-13a5-5d7b-8b8d-103e6b378823" :question/id #uuid "f427e4e2-13a5-5d7b-8b8d-103e6b378823" :question/math-academy-id "q-79320" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-29131f28-d633-5715-a931-1a4a0aae454c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-29131f28-d633-5715-a931-1a4a0aae454c" :answer-field/id #uuid "29131f28-d633-5715-a931-1a4a0aae454c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cbcba230-afee-51b7-a5c4-70037d514241" :answer-field/choices ["answer-73c49f7c-a1f3-56e9-95d3-4e8d7959ede8" "answer-bc38effb-2332-5570-8872-70a5b22fb92e" "answer-93ff253e-bb05-5ca6-8757-db5007d7fe23" "answer-cbcba230-afee-51b7-a5c4-70037d514241" "answer-9b73c0f1-2d21-5d2b-981e-9083bbda05f0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73c49f7c-a1f3-56e9-95d3-4e8d7959ede8" :answer/id #uuid "73c49f7c-a1f3-56e9-95d3-4e8d7959ede8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc38effb-2332-5570-8872-70a5b22fb92e" :answer/id #uuid "bc38effb-2332-5570-8872-70a5b22fb92e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93ff253e-bb05-5ca6-8757-db5007d7fe23" :answer/id #uuid "93ff253e-bb05-5ca6-8757-db5007d7fe23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbcba230-afee-51b7-a5c4-70037d514241" :answer/id #uuid "cbcba230-afee-51b7-a5c4-70037d514241" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b73c0f1-2d21-5d2b-981e-9083bbda05f0" :answer/id #uuid "9b73c0f1-2d21-5d2b-981e-9083bbda05f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186348205 :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}+{v}_{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$\n\nII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\})=4$\n\nIII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w\\})=5$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$ is spanned by the vectors by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}.$ Moreover, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$, and these are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\})=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}+{v}_{4}.$ Hence,$w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}.$ this means that $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w\\}$ is completely spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w\\})=4$ (not $5$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-948a33eb-6d1f-58c5-9917-674dc68468c6" :activity/id #uuid "948a33eb-6d1f-58c5-9917-674dc68468c6" :activity/title "The Dimension of a Span" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048218 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-5ee4ca5d-e09c-5ab8-a217-675407324399" "step-166d7282-f4b0-5e00-b772-331ab9716c49" "step-0af671dd-0c3a-50f4-a41a-e189faada01e" "step-8b0be1b0-7fd8-51b2-a3b6-8c